rules_version = '2';

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Cloud Storage rules — deny all client access.
//
// This app has no Firebase Auth. The browser therefore holds no credential
// Storage could authorise against, so there is no rule that admits the admin
// form without also admitting the entire internet. Rather than write one,
// the browser is cut out of Storage completely:
//
//   • Uploads  → POST /api/upload-image, which checks the admin session
//                cookie and writes with the Admin SDK.
//   • Reads    → the Firebase download-token URL returned by that route.
//
// Both paths bypass these rules. The Admin SDK authenticates as the service
// account, and download-token URLs are served by the Storage API without
// consulting rules at all — the unguessable token *is* the read credential.
//
// Verified 2026-09-20 against the live bucket: with these same denials in
// force (the lapsed starter rules), a token URL returned 200 OK while the
// client SDK returned storage/unauthorized for both read and write. Serving
// images needs nothing from this file.
//
// What replaced what: the rules deployed 2026-03-17 were the stock starter
// template, `allow read, write: if request.time < timestamp.date(2026, 4, 16)`.
// That expired on 2026-04-16 and had been denying all client traffic since —
// silently, because the admin form's upload failure was console-only.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

service firebase.storage {
  match /b/{bucket}/o {
    match /{allPaths=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
